ing Fractions by Swapping Denominators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050927 #edb/ref 1033 2459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050927 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7275db81-0f7a-5732-8201-28cd81b685b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142622 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142622 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n![](images/80/805043dc7270f411d6abb0edde2d24c79b9a700fddbab6f057cf010f688d528a.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142622 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "294b83f4-2e38-5bf4-a521-01fe8ca13fe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142622 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write ![](images/f3/f30ed07403432a8c183dd7a486efc7de50b792f92290af3fb54c70190f755da0.svg) as an improper fraction:\n\n![](images/ea/ea2c8853ff746445bea369a9ed84d0e70bac867b06e5a8654b0cb916bdf87da8.svg)\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n![](images/f2/f23b3a3dbbadfad8feda12f14cbadf615b49835464b98684b931c6aee5416ec3.svg)\n\nTherefore, the missing number is ![](images/e0/e008b7f9fc7f00e35668031c5cdc99067adc66c79ce950e5e4afb35f7cb9644d.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142624 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142624 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a688fc95-6831-57c6-9fee-f3295412af05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142624 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $2 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 2) + 1}{2} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} =  \\dfrac{1 \\times 5}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{5}{6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142626 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142626 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the missing number in the following equality.\n\n![](images/7a/7aee3025661cf0e0b7fdf5699d8a05dbbd6a2a9a6f8040d6835cc2ec7543a1d7.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142626 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "689fd963-ba93-5b0a-b7e4-0b8fd1dc9101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142626 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write ![](images/e5/e5aaba9db7118349d6239ec5528b38f96038715ceb4d857bafb24cbdf7752f37.svg) as an improper fraction:\n\n![](images/46/46ebc0b6db840b22d324d6a247052efd4da7aa43778418287b905bef47bf001a.svg)\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n![](images/1f/1ff2d2bddeb4958b37e00d1808cb639dbe1d0fb321ec615880a2ac669d65c02a.svg)\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n![](images/f7/f7a7753e17912b5638bdfcfdac2706a0c8e4f49a9be350b705a1d31a3fba4403.svg)\n\nHence, the missing number is ![](images/e0/e008b7f9fc7f00e35668031c5cdc99067adc66c79ce950e5e4afb35f7cb9644d.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142627 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142627 #edb/ref 1071 "Multiply $1 \\, \\dfrac{7}{8} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3} .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db3701cf-af70-508a-a158-5e38ff68246f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142627 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $1 \\,\\dfrac{7}{8}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\dfrac{7}{8} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 8) + 7}{8} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{8}\\times \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{8}\\times \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{15}{3}\\times \\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =5\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759