ers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{22}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{22\\times 1}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{22}{15}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{22}{15}=1\\,\\text{R}7=1\\,\\frac{7}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433556 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433556 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{4}{5}\\times 2\\,\\frac{1}{7}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "259c5182-ecae-5a4c-9e03-ff3c7b2b270c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433556 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{7}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{(2\\times 7)+1}{7}=\\frac{15}{7}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{15}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{15}{7} & =\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\text{ }3 \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{7}=1\\,\\text{R}5=1\\,\\frac{5}{7}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433557 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433557 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433557 #edb/ref 1071 "$1\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{2}{3}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fb302d8-e54d-5bd0-98ff-6702cdefa4b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433557 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+3}{5}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{5}\\times \\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{8\\times 2}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{16}{15}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{15}=1\\,\\text{R}1=1\\,\\frac{1}{15}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-57114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433558 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the missing number in the following equality.\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\times \\frac{6}{7}=1\\,\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{35}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7104d34b-6e4e-5044-bbe9-614ca52dfdce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433558 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+1}{5}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{5}\\times \\frac{6}{7}=\\frac{6\\times 6}{5\\times 7}=\\frac{36}{35}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{36}{35}=1\\,\\text{R}1=1\\,\\frac{1}{35}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-198150" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433559 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a fraction in lowest terms, $\\frac{2}{7}\\times 3\\,\\frac{1}{3}=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0a94de2-ad1f-55ec-8c96-c8bb2bd3533f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433559 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction